From 5a68cf4ea3589c9723d1670879fb1b0b968f1f55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:17:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Shield blockability from vanilla data and the pure shield
 rules

ShieldBlockability answers "would this shield stop that hit" from the game's own data: any item with the BLOCKS_ATTACKS component is a shield; the direct hit of each vanilla projectile (damage type and amount, read off the projectile classes of the 1.21.11 jar) goes through the shield's bypassed_by tag (#bypasses_shield) on the real registry, the Piercing rule of LivingEntity.applyItemBlocking, and a zero-damage hit is nothing to block (Player.hurtServer returns at once for it). The front arc is the widest horizontal_blocking_angle of the damage reductions that apply to the source, the delay block_delay_seconds.

Verified against the decompiled jar (scratch only): snowballs, eggs and ender pearls deal a thrown hit of 0 to a player (a snowball hurts only blazes; the ender_pearl damage type is the thrower's own landing), so they are never reacted to (the spec listed snowballs and eggs as blockable; the jar says there is nothing to block). A guardian beam (Guardian.GuardianAttackGoal) deals indirect_magic (1, 3 on Hard, +2 elder: unblockable) and then doHurtTarget's mob_attack (6, elder 8), which the shield blocks from the front: the larger part is blockable, so the beam is blocked like a melee hit. Splash and lingering potions and evoker fangs (indirect_magic), dragon fireballs (no hit; the cloud is magic), sonic_boom, lightning_bolt and the environment types of the tag stay unblockable.

ShieldRules holds the pure decisions: the head-relative front arc and the turn at the human rate, whether a raise can be active before a hit (turn and block delay together, a hotbar change first, one tick of slack), the reaction rule for a projectile (a shooter being tracked was anticipated; anything else waits the shared perception formula), the hand policy (eating, a draw or another use is cancelled only for a lethal hit), the main-hand use order and the melee rhythm state machine.

Tests: ShieldBlockabilityTest reads #bypasses_shield (with #bypasses_armor) from the vanilla jar and checks every projectile and damage kind, the zero-damage throws, the Piercing rule and the guardian beam; ShieldRulesTest covers the arc, the turn, the block delay, the reaction timing (an arrow from an unnoticed shooter at 15 blocks lands before the 0.85 s reaction; a ghast fireball seen at 40 blocks is still blocked), the hand policy, the main-hand order and a full swing/raise/hold/lower cycle with and without a disabled shield.

